Build a Production-Quality UI Framework for a Minecraft SkyBlock Mod

Build a reusable, production-quality UI framework for a modern Minecraft SkyBlock mod.

The framework must support:

1. Full configuration screens
2. In-game HUD elements
3. Temporary screen-space overlays
4. Optional world-space overlays
5. A visual HUD editor

This must be a reusable framework, not a hard-coded configuration menu or a collection of unrelated HUD implementations.

⸻

1. Mandatory implementation strategy

Do not attempt to implement the entire framework in one undifferentiated pass.

Work in the ordered phases defined below.

At the end of each phase:

1. Ensure the project compiles.
2. Run all relevant tests.
3. Document architectural decisions.
4. Demonstrate the completed phase.
5. Do not begin the next phase until the previous phase has working acceptance criteria.

Prioritize correctness of the core runtime over the number of implemented widgets.

A smaller set of complete, extensible, tested components is preferable to a large collection of incomplete components.

⸻

2. Platform assumptions

Use these assumptions unless the existing project explicitly requires alternatives:

* Language: Kotlin
* Java compatibility: Java 21
* Build system: Gradle Kotlin DSL
* Mod loader: Fabric
* Minecraft version: define one explicit supported version in the project
* Mappings: Yarn
* Rendering: Minecraft GUI rendering through an adapter
* Mixins: permitted only where required for lifecycle or rendering integration
* Serialization: kotlinx.serialization
* Tests: JUnit 5
* Coroutines: permitted outside rendering hot paths
* No blocking file I/O on the client render thread
* No external retained-mode UI toolkit
* Minecraft classes must remain outside the framework-independent core wherever practical

If adapting to another loader or Minecraft version, isolate the changes inside the Minecraft adapter module.

Before implementation, record the exact:

* Minecraft version
* Fabric Loader version
* Fabric API version
* Kotlin version
* Java version
* Gradle version
* Mapping version

⸻

3. Design language

The supplied visual references establish the default design language:

* Clean
* Minimal
* Premium
* Dark translucent surfaces
* Near-black navy backgrounds
* Slightly lighter elevated cards
* Thin soft-gray borders
* Restrained purple accents around #8B5CF6
* Rounded corners
* Clear compact typography
* Compact controls
* Subtle shadows
* Minimal blur
* No excessive glow
* No large distracting panels
* No unnecessary decoration
* No visual clutter

Configuration screens may use structured panels.

Normal HUD elements must remain compact and focused. A HUD should usually communicate one purpose rather than behave like a full-screen dashboard.

Do not begin by hard-coding the supplied visual references. Build the framework primitives and theme tokens first.

⸻

4. Core objective

The framework must make normal configuration and HUD creation concise while retaining enough flexibility for:

* Large configuration systems
* Custom components
* Custom layouts
* Third-party modules
* Multiple HUD instances
* Dynamic data
* Future mod features
* Minecraft-specific controls
* World-space overlays
* Custom themes
* Profile-aware persistence

Developers should be able to declaratively describe:

* Screens
* Categories
* Sections
* Settings
* Layouts
* Bindings
* Validation
* Visibility conditions
* Themes
* HUD definitions
* HUD instances
* Preview data
* Notifications
* World overlays

The framework should automatically handle:

* Rendering
* Layout
* Input
* Focus
* Hover and pressed states
* Scrolling
* Search
* Tooltips
* Keyboard navigation
* Validation
* Persistence
* Profiles
* Import and export
* Undo and redo
* Animation
* Responsive sizing
* HUD dragging
* HUD scaling
* HUD anchoring
* HUD persistence
* HUD editor integration
* Resolution changes
* Minecraft GUI-scale changes

Normal API usage must not require manual child-coordinate calculation.

⸻

5. Explicit non-goals for the initial release

The following must not block the stable initial release:

* Full right-to-left layout support
* Controller navigation
* Arbitrary graph editing
* Timeline editors
* General-purpose rule builders
* Full nested-object form generation
* Nested HUD groups
* Arbitrary rotation of HUD elements
* Rich document editing
* Plugin-defined shader pipelines
* Cross-device cloud synchronization
* Collaborative configuration editing

Design extension points so these can be added later, but do not sacrifice core correctness to implement them early.

⸻

6. Required architectural decisions

Before implementing visual components, create an architecture document that resolves the following.

6.1 State model

Use a typed reactive state system with explicit ownership.

Define:

* Mutable state
* Read-only state
* Derived state
* Subscriptions
* Invalidation
* Update policies
* Thread confinement
* Snapshot semantics
* Disposal
* Dependency tracking
* Dependency-cycle detection

Do not rely exclusively on arbitrary lambdas being reevaluated every frame.

A condition such as:

visibleWhen = config.showNotificationsState

should be preferred over an opaque lambda that gives the runtime no dependency information.

Convenience lambda APIs may exist, but the reliable core must use explicit typed dependencies or tracked reactive reads.

6.2 Rendering model

Use a documented hybrid retained model.

The implementation must clearly define:

* Which objects persist between frames
* Which render nodes are ephemeral
* How component identity is preserved
* Where temporary component state is stored
* How dirty-state invalidation works
* When layout measurement occurs
* When nodes are materialized
* How virtualized rows are created and discarded
* How focus survives virtualization
* How animations survive target changes

Do not rebuild the complete component tree every frame.

6.3 Threading model

Define a strict threading contract.

At minimum:

* Rendering and input dispatch occur on the Minecraft client thread.
* Minecraft object access occurs on the client thread unless explicitly documented safe.
* Background persistence operates on immutable snapshots.
* Background work submits results through a client-thread scheduler.
* UI state updates from other threads must use an explicit thread-safe submission API.
* Callbacks must document which thread invokes them.

6.4 Extension ownership

All runtime registrations must belong to a registration scope or owner token.

This includes:

* Components
* Renderers
* Validators
* Serializers
* Icons
* HUD definitions
* Search providers
* Notification types
* World overlays
* Context-menu actions

When a module unloads, disposing its registration scope must remove its registrations and release retained references.

Avoid leaking:

* Lambdas from unloaded modules
* Classloaders
* Active HUD instances
* Cached renderer objects
* Search-index entries
* Icons
* serializers

