The exact structure may be adjusted, but these boundaries must remain clear:

* Stable public API
* Framework-independent runtime
* Standard components
* Minecraft-specific integration
* HUD editor
* Persistence
* Testing utilities
* Demonstrations

Provide a dependency graph.

Module dependencies must be acyclic.

Implementation classes must not leak into stable public APIs unless they are deliberate extension points.

⸻

8. Stable identifiers

Do not use unqualified strings as the primary identifier system.

Use namespaced stable identifiers.

Example:

@JvmInline
value class UiId private constructor(val value: String) {
    companion object {
        fun of(namespace: String, path: String): UiId
        fun parse(value: String): UiId
    }
}

Examples:

skyblockmod:general.notifications
skyblockmod:hud.mining_xp
thirdpartymod:widget.gradient

Identifiers must be validated.

Detect:

* Duplicate setting IDs
* Duplicate HUD definition IDs
* Duplicate instance IDs
* Duplicate component registrations
* Invalid namespace syntax
* Invalid path syntax

Serialized enum-like values must use stable explicit IDs rather than enum ordinal positions.

⸻

9. Public API design

Design the public API before implementing the standard visual library.

Public APIs must exist for:

* Screen definitions
* Configuration settings
* Typed state
* Typed bindings
* Layout primitives
* Components
* Renderers
* Themes
* Validators
* Serializers
* Profile stores
* HUD definitions
* HUD instances
* HUD layout stores
* Icons
* Search providers
* Notifications
* World overlays
* Extension registration scopes

Avoid exposing internal runtime classes.

Kotlin must receive a concise DSL.

Java consumers must receive a fluent API over the same core model.

⸻

10. Configuration model

The configuration model is the durable source of truth.

It must not contain transient rendering state.

Every setting must support:

* Stable namespaced ID
* Display name
* Description
* Optional tooltip
* Category
* Section
* Typed default value
* Typed current-value binding
* Optional icon
* Search keywords
* Visibility state
* Enabled state
* Validation rules
* Change events
* Reset behavior
* Optional restart-required flag
* Optional experimental flag
* Optional warning
* Optional confirmation requirement
* Optional permission requirement

Do not use raw Any, arbitrary object maps, or untyped values as the primary API.

Temporary state such as hover, focus, selection, open dropdown state, cursor position, drag state, and animation progress must live in runtime-owned state stores.

⸻

11. Declarative configuration DSL

Normal settings must require only a few lines.

Target style:

val screen = configScreen(
    id = UiId.of("skyblockmod", "main"),
    title = "SkyBlock Mod Config"
) {
    category(
        id = UiId.of("skyblockmod", "general"),
        title = "General",
        icon = Icons.settings
    ) {
        section("Interface") {
            dropdown(
                id = UiId.of("skyblockmod", "general.theme"),
                title = "Theme",
                description = "Choose your preferred theme",
                value = config.themeBinding,
                options = Theme.entries.map {
                    option(
                        id = it.serializedId,
                        label = it.displayName,
                        value = it
                    )
                }
            )
            colorPicker(
                id = UiId.of("skyblockmod", "general.accent_color"),
                title = "Accent Color",
                value = config.accentColorBinding
            )
            keybind(
                id = UiId.of("skyblockmod", "general.menu_keybind"),
                title = "Mod Menu Keybind",
                value = config.menuKeyBinding
            )
            toggle(
                id = UiId.of("skyblockmod", "general.notifications"),
                title = "Show In-Game Notifications",
                value = config.notificationsBinding
            )
        }
    }
}

The API must remain practical with hundreds or thousands of settings.

Developers must not instantiate low-level widget or renderer classes for standard controls.

⸻

12. Typed state and bindings

Provide typed two-way bindings.

Support:

* Mutable property references
* Getter and setter pairs
* Observable state
* Read-only state
* Derived state
* Transformations
* Validation
* Debouncing
* Change events
* Undo integration
* Explicit disposal

Example:

toggle(
    id = UiId.of("skyblockmod", "feature.enabled"),
    title = "Enabled",
    value = bind(
        get = config::enabled,
        set = config::setEnabled
    )
)

Reflection-based convenience may exist outside hot paths, but explicit typed bindings must be the reliable foundation.

Define binding failure behavior.

Broken bindings must produce clear development errors rather than silently failing.

⸻

13. Reactive behavior

Settings and HUDs must support:

* Conditional visibility
* Conditional enabled state
* Dynamic warnings
* Dynamic options
* Dynamic labels
* Dynamic descriptions
* Derived values
* Composable predicates
* Dependency graphs
* Cycle detection

Example:

slider(
    id = UiId.of("skyblockmod", "notifications.duration"),
    title = "Notification Duration",
    value = config.notificationDurationBinding,
    visibleWhen = config.showNotificationsState,
    enabledWhen = config.compactModeState.map { compact -> !compact }
)

Dependency cycles must produce a useful error containing the dependency path.

Updating one state value must not rebuild unrelated screens or HUD elements.

⸻

14. Component registry

Do not render component types through a giant conditional statement.

Create a typed component registry.

It must support:

* Built-in components
* Third-party components
* Generic typed components
* Runtime registration
* Safe unregistration
* Custom renderers
* Custom serializers
* Custom validators
* Search indexing
* Editor integrations
* Registration ownership

Example:

registrationScope.registerComponent<GradientSetting>(
    renderer = GradientSettingRenderer(),
    serializer = GradientSettingSerializer(),
    validator = GradientSettingValidator()
)

Custom controls must be registerable without modifying core framework source.

Missing renderers must display a developer-facing fallback instead of crashing the entire screen.

⸻

15. Layout engine

Create a real layout engine.

Support:

* Vertical stacks
* Horizontal rows
* Overlays
* Grids
* Cards
* Sections
* Dividers
* Padding
* Margins
* Insets
* Alignment
* Minimum sizes
* Maximum sizes
* Fixed sizes
* Flexible growth
* Text wrapping
* Responsive columns
* Collapsible groups
* Scroll containers
* Nested scroll containers only where justified
* Sticky headers where practical

The standard API must not expose raw child coordinates.

The layout engine must adapt to:

* 1280×720
* 1920×1080
* 2560×1440
* Ultrawide displays
* Small resizable windows
* Minecraft GUI-scale changes
* Long translated strings
* Larger accessibility text
* Optional inspectors and sidebars

Implement dirty measurement and layout invalidation.

Do not remeasure static subtrees without cause.

⸻

16. Virtualization

Large configuration systems must be virtualized.

Only visible elements plus a configurable overscan region should be:

* Materialized where possible
* Fully measured
* Rendered
* Included in expensive hit testing
* Actively animated

Support:

* Thousands of settings
* Dynamic row heights
* Cached row measurements
* Incremental measurement updates
* Scroll-to-item
* Focus restoration
* Search-result navigation
* Temporarily expanded parent sections

The runtime must distinguish:

* Registered elements
* Materialized elements
* Measured elements
* Visible elements
* Animated elements
* Hit-test candidates

⸻

17. Rendering abstraction

Configuration models and HUD definitions must not depend on Minecraft drawing classes.

Provide an abstraction similar to:

interface UiRenderer {
    fun fillRect(bounds: Rect, color: Color)
    fun roundedRect(bounds: Rect, radius: Dp, color: Color)
    fun border(bounds: Rect, radius: Dp, width: Dp, color: Color)
    fun text(layout: TextLayout, position: Vec2, color: Color)
    fun icon(icon: Icon, bounds: Rect, tint: Color)
    fun image(texture: TextureRef, bounds: Rect, tint: Color)
    fun pushClip(bounds: Rect)
    fun popClip()
    fun pushTransform(transform: Transform)
    fun popTransform()
    fun pushOpacity(opacity: Float)
    fun popOpacity()
}

Support:

* Rectangles
* Rounded rectangles
* Borders
* Text
* Icons
* Images
* Clipping
* Scissor regions
* Controlled blur
* Shadows
* Gradients
* Transforms
* Opacity
* Frame timing

Minecraft rendering must be implemented through an adapter.

Use batching where possible.

Cache:

* Text measurement
* Text layouts
* Icon lookups
* Static geometry
* Theme token resolution

Avoid unnecessary render-state changes.

⸻

18. Input system

Centralize input handling.

Support:

* Mouse movement
* Mouse buttons
* Mouse wheel
* Dragging
* Keyboard input
* Character input
* Keybind capture
* Focus traversal
* Tab navigation
* Enter and space activation
* Escape behavior
* Click-outside behavior
* Modal dialogs
* Context menus
* Tooltips
* Pointer capture

Use explicit event propagation:

* Capture
* Target
* Bubble
* Consumed events
* Focus owner
* Pointer owner

Components must not independently poll global mouse state.

Scaled HUD hit testing must transform input coordinates correctly.

⸻

19. Theme system

Use a token-based theme system.

Do not hard-code colors, spacing, radii, typography, or animation durations inside components.

Tokens must include:

* Window background
* Panel background
* Elevated panel background
* Hover background
* Pressed background
* Border colors
* Accent
* Success
* Warning
* Error
* Primary text
* Secondary text
* Disabled text
* Corner radii
* Shadows
* Blur strength
* Spacing scale
* Typography scale
* Animation durations
* Control heights
* Sidebar width
* Inspector width

Support:

* Dark theme
* Light theme
* Custom themes
* Runtime theme switching
* Custom accent colors
* High contrast
* Reduced motion
* Larger text

Do not rely on color alone to communicate state.

⸻

20. Animation system

Animations must be:

* Delta-time based
* Interruptible
* Target-driven
* Reversible
* Disposable
* Reduced-motion aware

Support:

* Hover transitions
* Toggle movement
* Dropdown opening
* Accordion expansion
* Selection highlights
* Focus indicators
* Modal transitions
* Scroll interpolation
* HUD fade
* HUD slide
* Numeric interpolation
* Progress interpolation
* Temporary highlighting

When a target changes mid-animation, continue smoothly from the current value.

Do not restart from the original value.

Avoid animating large blur regions every frame.

⸻

21. Validation

Provide typed validation.

Support:

* Required values
* Numeric ranges
* String lengths
* Regex
* Custom validators
* Cross-field validation
* Warnings
* Errors
* Optional asynchronous validation

Invalid values must not silently corrupt persisted configuration.

Validation results must contain:

* Severity
* Clear message
* Relevant field ID
* Optional remediation
* Optional related field IDs

⸻

22. Persistence and profiles

Use storage abstractions.

interface ConfigStore {
    suspend fun load(profileId: ProfileId): ConfigSnapshot
    suspend fun save(profileId: ProfileId, snapshot: ConfigSnapshot)
}
interface HudLayoutStore {
    suspend fun load(profileId: ProfileId): HudLayoutSnapshot
    suspend fun save(profileId: ProfileId, snapshot: HudLayoutSnapshot)
}

Support:

* Default profile
* Create
* Rename
* Duplicate
* Delete
* Switch
* Optional per-server profiles
* Optional per-SkyBlock-profile resolution
* Import
* Export
* Reset setting
* Reset section
* Reset category
* Reset everything
* Schema versioning
* Migrations
* Atomic writes
* Backup creation
* Corruption recovery
* Unknown-field preservation where practical

Saving must:

* Be debounced
* Run outside the render path
* Use immutable snapshots
* Write atomically
* Recover from partial writes where possible

Feature configuration and HUD layout should be stored separately.

⸻

23. Undo and change tracking

Support optional undo and redo.

Track:

* Individual setting changes
* List edits
* Reordering
* Reset operations
* Bulk changes
* HUD movement
* HUD scaling
* HUD resizing
* HUD grouping
* HUD visibility
* HUD z-order
* HUD lock state

Support interaction modes:

* Immediate persistence
* Apply and cancel
* Save and close

Expose whether unsaved changes exist.

Continuous drag operations should collapse into one undo action rather than producing an entry for every mouse movement.

⸻

24. Search

Implement global configuration search.

Index:

* Setting title
* Description
* Category
* Section
* Keywords
* Option labels
* Aliases
* Stable ID

Search results must:

* Rank exact and prefix matches highly
* Support predictable fuzzy matching
* Highlight matching text
* Display category paths
* Navigate directly to settings
* Expand collapsed parents temporarily
* Scroll virtualized settings into view

Search indexes must update incrementally.

Do not rebuild the full index every frame.

Provide deterministic ranking tests.

