26. In-game HUD system

HUD elements must be first-class framework definitions.

Do not implement HUDs as unrelated custom callbacks with manual coordinate, scale, drag, and persistence logic.

A HUD definition must support:

* Stable namespaced ID
* Title
* Category
* Default anchor
* Default offset
* Default scale
* Scale range
* Optional size constraints
* Visibility state
* Data source
* Declarative layout
* Optional settings
* Preview data
* Update policies
* Layer
* Search metadata

The framework must automatically provide:

* Position persistence
* Scale persistence
* Optional resize persistence
* Anchoring
* Dragging
* Selection
* Alignment guides
* Snapping
* Bounding boxes
* Z-order
* Visibility
* Reset behavior
* Profile support
* Resolution adaptation
* GUI-scale adaptation
* Editor integration

Developers must not manually implement editor dragging, scaling, coordinate conversion, or serialization for standard HUDs.

⸻

27. HUD API

Target API:

hudElement(
    id = UiId.of("skyblockmod", "hud.mining_xp"),
    title = "Mining XP",
    defaultAnchor = Anchor.BOTTOM_CENTER,
    defaultOffset = Vec2(0f, -90f),
    defaultScale = 1f,
    scaleRange = 0.5f..2.5f,
    visibleWhen = skyblockState.isMining
) {
    card {
        row(
            verticalAlignment = Alignment.CENTER,
            spacing = 10.dp
        ) {
            itemIcon(miningSkillIconState)
            column(
                grow = true,
                spacing = 4.dp
            ) {
                row {
                    text("Mining XP", style = TextStyle.Title)
                    spacer(grow = true)
                    text(
                        value = formattedXpState,
                        style = TextStyle.Secondary
                    )
                }
                progressBar(
                    value = progressState,
                    accent = theme.accent
                )
            }
        }
    }
}

Provide specialized builders over the same runtime:

progressHud(
    id = UiId.of("skyblockmod", "hud.mining_xp"),
    title = "Mining XP",
    icon = Icons.mining,
    current = state.currentXp,
    maximum = state.requiredXp,
    subtitle = state.level.map { "Lv. $it" }
)

Specialized builders must compose standard primitives rather than use a separate rendering implementation.

⸻

28. Reactive HUD state

Support:

* Observable values
* Derived values
* Event-driven updates
* Tick-based updates
* Fixed intervals
* Lazy values
* Cached values
* Debounced values
* Manual invalidation
* Thread-safe submissions

Per-value update policies:

text(
    value = session.coinsPerHour,
    updatePolicy = UpdatePolicy.Interval(500.milliseconds)
)

Supported policies:

* On change
* Every tick
* Every frame
* Fixed interval
* Manual

Every-frame updates must be opt-in.

Updating one HUD value must not invalidate unrelated HUDs.

⸻

29. HUD context and lifecycle

HUD elements must receive explicit context.

interface HudContext {
    val screenSize: Size
    val guiScale: Float
    val partialTick: Float
    val player: PlayerContext?
    val skyblock: SkyblockContext?
    val theme: HudTheme
}

Define a lifecycle:

* Registered
* Initialized
* Activated
* Updated
* Measured
* Rendered
* Deactivated
* Disposed

Avoid global static access from HUD implementations.

⸻

30. HUD anchoring and placement

Do not persist only absolute pixels.

Support:

* Top left
* Top center
* Top right
* Center left
* Center
* Center right
* Bottom left
* Bottom center
* Bottom right

Persist:

data class HudPlacement(
    val anchor: Anchor,
    val offset: Vec2,
    val scale: Float,
    val size: Size?,
    val zIndex: Int,
    val opacity: Float,
    val locked: Boolean
)

HUDs must preserve their relationship to screen edges when:

* Resolution changes
* Window size changes
* GUI scale changes

Changing an anchor in the editor must preserve the HUD’s visible screen position.

Add exhaustive anchor-conversion tests.

⸻

31. HUD editor

The game world must remain visible behind a subtle dim overlay.

The editor must support:

* Move HUDs
* Scale HUDs
* Resize supported HUDs
* Enable and disable HUDs
* Reset HUDs
* Change anchors
* Change z-order
* Align HUDs
* Snap HUDs
* Group HUDs
* Lock HUDs
* Preview inactive HUDs
* Search HUDs
* Filter by category or module
* Edit HUD-specific settings
* Undo and redo

Do not obscure the screen with a large opaque interface.

Selection

Support:

* Single selection
* Shift-click multi-selection
* Drag-selection rectangle
* Select all
* Deselect
* Cycling overlapping elements

Selected HUDs display:

* Subtle outline
* Bounding box
* Scale handles
* Name
* Scale
* Anchor
* Optional dimensions
* Compact contextual toolbar

Dragging

Support:

* Free dragging
* Modifier to disable snapping
* Precision modifier
* Arrow-key nudging
* Larger modified nudging
* Boundary clamping
* Optional off-screen placement
* Multi-element movement
* Anchor-relative movement

The cursor must remain aligned at every scale.

Scaling and resizing

Treat scaling and resizing as different operations.

Scaling changes the rendered output through transforms.

Resizing changes available layout space and may trigger wrapping or reflow.

HUDs may support:

* Scaling only
* Resizing only
* Both
* Neither

Support:

* Corner scale handles
* Mouse-wheel scaling while selected
* Numeric scale input
* Scale presets
* Reset scale
* Minimum and maximum scale
* Aspect-ratio locking
* Multi-selection scaling

⸻

32. Snapping, guides, and safe areas

Display guides for:

* Screen center
* Screen edges
* Other HUD edges
* Other HUD centers
* Safe margins
* Hotbar
* Chat
* Scoreboard
* Boss bars

Support snapping to:

* Grid
* Screen center
* Safe areas
* Other HUDs
* Vanilla UI boundaries

Visualize optional safe areas for:

* Hotbar
* Crosshair
* Chat
* Scoreboard
* Boss bars
* Status effects
* Action bar
* Titles
* Subtitles

Snapping thresholds must be configurable.

Warn about critical overlap, but do not force prevention unless configured.

⸻

33. HUD inspector

When a HUD is selected, show a compact inspector with:

* Name
* Enabled state
* Anchor
* X offset
* Y offset
* Scale
* Width
* Height
* Opacity
* Z-index
* Lock state
* Reset action
* HUD-specific settings
* Delete action for user-created instances

Support direct numeric input.

Changes should apply immediately.

HUD-specific settings must be usable both in the normal configuration screen and in the HUD inspector from the same underlying setting definitions.

⸻

34. Multiple HUD instances

Some HUD definitions must support multiple instances.

Examples:

* Timers
* Waypoints
* Counters
* Custom text
* Stat displays

Each instance must have independent:

* Instance ID
* Placement
* Scale
* Size
* Settings
* Visibility
* Data binding
* Z-order
* Lock state

Example:

hudManager.createInstance(
    definitionId = UiId.of("skyblockmod", "hud.stat_display"),
    instanceId = UiId.of("skyblockmod", "hud.instance.damage_per_second")
)

⸻

35. HUD visibility

Support composable, testable visibility rules:

* Only in SkyBlock
* Only on selected islands
* Only in dungeons
* Only during Kuudra
* Only while holding an item
* Only while a feature is active
* Hide while chat is open
* Hide while the debug screen is open
* Hide in screenshots
* Show only when data is meaningful
* Show only in the editor

Visibility logic must not be embedded directly in render methods.

⸻

36. Notifications

Provide a notification stack for:

* Rare drops
* Coin gains
* Ability ready
* Warnings
* Errors
* Quest updates
* Party invitations
* Cooldown completion

Example:

notifications.show(
    Notification.success(
        title = "Rare Drop!",
        message = "You found a Wither Essence",
        icon = Icons.rareDrop,
        duration = 4.seconds
    )
)

Support:

* Position
* Queueing
* Stacking
* Maximum visible count
* Deduplication
* Priority
* Dismiss actions
* Duration progress
* Action buttons
* Hover pause
* Custom types

Notifications should use a persistent notification-region definition rather than every notification being independently positioned in the HUD editor.

⸻

37. World-space overlays

Provide a separate API for world-space content.

Examples:

* Waypoints
* Entity labels
* Block labels
* Objective markers
* Route nodes
* Distance markers

Example:

worldOverlay(
    id = UiId.of("skyblockmod", "overlay.commission_waypoint"),
    position = commission.targetPosition
) {
    marker(Icons.waypoint)
    text(commission.targetName)
    distance(player.distanceTo(commission.targetPosition))
}

Support:

* Depth-testing modes
* Maximum render distance
* Distance scaling
* Occlusion behavior
* Screen-edge indicators
* Screen clamping
* Distance fading
* Click-through behavior

Do not mix world-space placement logic with screen-space HUD placement.

⸻

38. Performance budgets

Target 60 FPS during normal gameplay on representative mid-range hardware.

Document the test hardware.

For the stress-test configuration screen, target:

* Idle update time: under 1 ms average
* Visible layout time: under 2 ms average
* Visible render preparation: under 2 ms average
* Search response for 5,000 settings: under 50 ms
* No blocking persistence on the render thread
* No complete tree rebuild during idle frames
* No unbounded per-frame allocation growth

For HUD stress testing with 50 visible simple HUDs:

* HUD update time: under 1 ms average
* HUD layout time: under 1.5 ms average
* HUD render preparation: under 1.5 ms average

These are target budgets, not permission to hide measurements.

If a target cannot be achieved, report the measured result and bottleneck honestly.

Provide debug metrics for:

* Configuration update time
* Layout time
* Render time
* HUD update time
* HUD layout time
* HUD render time
* Active HUD count
* Visible HUD count
* Materialized component count
* Visible component count
* Hit-test candidate count
* Draw-call count
* Text-layout count
* Cache hit rate
* Allocation rate where measurable

⸻

39. Failure modes to avoid

Do not build the framework around:

* One giant renderer when or switch statement
* One monolithic configuration-screen class
* Raw Any values
* Untyped maps as the primary model
* Persistent values mixed with animation or focus state
* Raw screen pixels as the only HUD placement system
* Full processing of every setting each frame
* Full component-tree rebuilding every frame
* Global mouse polling inside components
* Rendering tightly coupled to configuration storage
* Reflection in hot paths
* File I/O on the render thread
* Manual per-HUD dragging implementations
* Manual per-HUD scale persistence
* Manual child-coordinate calculation
* Unowned global registries
* Static global HUD context
* Enum ordinal persistence
* Silent binding failures
* Silent validation failures
* Catch-all exception swallowing
* Placeholder tests that only verify object construction

⸻

40. Error handling

Detect and report:

* Duplicate setting IDs
* Duplicate HUD IDs
* Duplicate instance IDs
* Missing renderers
* Invalid ranges
* Invalid defaults
* Dependency cycles
* Broken bindings
* Unsupported serialized values
* Invalid schemas
* Invalid HUD layouts
* Layout overflow
* Missing icons
* Component exceptions
* Invalid anchor data
* Invalid scale values
* Missing preview data where required
* Registration ownership violations

One broken custom component or HUD should not necessarily crash the full screen.

Where practical, render a development error placeholder containing:

* Component ID
* Component type
* Concise error
* Link or action to inspect diagnostics

Do not expose raw stack traces to normal users by default.

⸻

41. Developer tooling

Include:

* UI inspector
* Component-tree viewer
* HUD-tree viewer
* Bounds visualization
* Focus visualization
* Hitbox visualization
* Layout timing
* Render timing
* Update timing
* Search-index viewer
* Theme-token viewer
* Current hovered component
* Current focused component
* Current selected HUD
* Anchor visualization
* Safe-area visualization
* Reload UI command
* Component gallery
* HUD gallery
* Stress-test mode

Development tooling must be disableable in production builds.

⸻

42. Testing strategy

Pure JVM tests

Use framework-independent tests for:

* Typed state
* Bindings
* Validation
* Serialization
* Schema migrations
* Search ranking
* Conditional visibility
* Dependency-cycle detection
* List editing
* Undo and redo
* Focus traversal
* Hit testing
* Layout calculations
* Virtualization
* Profile switching
* Import and export
* Component registration
* HUD registration
* Anchor conversion
* Resolution changes
* GUI-scale changes
* HUD dragging
* HUD scaling
* HUD resizing
* HUD snapping
* HUD grouping
* Multi-selection
* Z-order
* Visibility rules
* Preview state
* HUD persistence
* Multiple instances
* Safe-area warnings
* Scaled hit testing
* Registration disposal

Fake-platform integration tests

Create a fake renderer and fake input system.

Test:

* Complete render trees
* Event propagation
* Focus behavior
* Pointer capture
* Clipping
* Modal behavior
* Virtualized navigation
* Editor transformations
* Animation interruption

Visual tests

Add screenshot or snapshot tests at:

* 1280×720
* 1920×1080
* 2560×1440
* Ultrawide
* High GUI scale
* Low GUI scale
* Large text mode
* High-contrast mode
* Reduced-motion mode

Minecraft integration tests

Add smoke tests for:

* Opening the configuration screen
* Rendering standard components
* Input forwarding
* HUD lifecycle
* Resolution changes
* GUI-scale changes
* World-overlay rendering
* Resource reloads
* Module registration and unregistration

Tests must verify behavior, not merely successful construction.

⸻

43. Stress tests

Create a stress-test screen containing at least:

* 1,000 toggles
* 500 sliders
* 200 dropdowns
* Several large editable lists
* Nested conditional sections
* Dozens of active HUDs
* Several animated values
* Multiple world overlays

Measure:

* Frame time
* Update time
* Layout time
* Render time
* Draw calls
* Registered elements
* Materialized elements
* Visible elements
* Hit-test candidates
* Allocation rate
* Cache hit rate

Include an automated benchmark report.

⸻

44. Required demonstrations

Create:

1. A configuration screen matching the supplied design language
2. A component gallery
3. A HUD editor
4. A mining XP HUD
5. A temporary-notification demo
6. A world-space waypoint demo
7. A stress-test screen

The mining XP HUD must contain:

* One compact horizontal card
* Dark translucent background
* Rounded corners
* Thin border
* Small icon block on the left
* Title and level text
* Current and required XP aligned on the right
* Thin purple progress bar
* No unrelated elements
* No large surrounding UI
* No distracting glow
* No screen takeover

The demo must prove that the HUD can be:

* Created declaratively
* Moved in the editor
* Scaled
* Anchored
* Persisted
* Previewed with fake data
* Updated with real reactive data
* Themed consistently with configuration screens

⸻

