⸻

46. Ordered implementation phases

Phase 0 — Architecture

Deliver:

* Platform-version matrix
* Dependency graph
* State-model design
* Rendering-model design
* Threading contract
* Extension ownership model
* Public API proposal
* Persistence format proposal
* Performance measurement plan

Do not implement the full component library yet.

Phase 1 — Core runtime

Implement:

* IDs
* Typed state
* Bindings
* Component identity
* Runtime tree
* Dirty invalidation
* Layout primitives
* Fake renderer
* Central input system
* Focus
* Theme tokens
* Basic animation
* Diagnostics

Acceptance criteria:

* Pure JVM tests pass
* Fake-renderer demo works
* No Minecraft dependency in core modules
* No full-tree rebuild during idle frames

Phase 2 — Configuration foundation

Implement:

* Screen DSL
* Categories
* Sections
* Scrolling
* Virtualization
* Search
* Validation
* Persistence
* Profiles
* Undo
* Required phase-one controls

Acceptance criteria:

* 1,000-toggle stress test remains responsive
* Search navigation works with virtualization
* Persistence is atomic and off-thread
* Keyboard navigation works

Phase 3 — Minecraft adapter

Implement:

* Minecraft renderer
* Font adapter
* Texture adapter
* Screen lifecycle
* Input forwarding
* Resource reload handling
* GUI-scale adaptation

Acceptance criteria:

* Demo screen renders in Minecraft
* Resolution and GUI-scale changes pass tests
* No file I/O occurs on the render thread

Phase 4 — HUD runtime

Implement:

* HUD definitions
* HUD instances
* HUD context
* Anchoring
* Placement
* Scaling
* Resizing
* Visibility
* Preview state
* Persistence
* HUD registry
* Basic HUD composition

Acceptance criteria:

* Mining XP HUD works
* Anchor changes preserve visible position
* Scaled hit testing passes
* One value update does not rebuild unrelated HUDs

Phase 5 — HUD editor

Implement:

* Selection
* Dragging
* Scaling
* Resizing
* Inspector
* Snapping
* Guides
* Safe areas
* Locking
* Z-order
* Multi-selection
* Undo integration

Acceptance criteria:

* Drag cursor alignment remains correct at all scales
* Multi-selection transformations are reversible
* Placement survives resolution and GUI-scale changes

Phase 6 — Notifications and world overlays

Implement:

* Notification region
* Notification queue
* World-overlay API
* Waypoint demo
* Distance fading
* Screen-edge indicators

Acceptance criteria:

* Screen-space and world-space placement remain separate
* Overlays dispose cleanly with their registration scope

Phase 7 — Extended components and tooling

Implement:

* Remaining standard controls
* Minecraft-specific selectors
* Component gallery
* Inspector tools
* Stress-test instrumentation
* Visual snapshot suite

Do not add advanced components before the earlier acceptance criteria pass.

⸻

47. Expected output

Deliver, in order:

1. Architecture overview
2. Platform assumptions
3. Dependency graph
4. Public API design
5. Core state model
6. Threading contract
7. Extension ownership model
8. Module structure
9. Core interfaces
10. Typed configuration model
11. Binding system
12. Component registry
13. Layout engine
14. Input system
15. Rendering abstraction
16. Theme system
17. Validation
18. Persistence and profiles
19. Search
20. Virtualization
21. Undo and redo
22. Standard component library
23. Minecraft adapter
24. HUD runtime
25. HUD registry
26. HUD editor
27. HUD inspector
28. Anchor and placement system
29. Snapping and guides
30. Notification system
31. World-overlay system
32. Demonstrations
33. Stress-test results
34. Automated tests
35. Documentation
36. Complete example usage

Begin with Phase 0: architecture and public API.

Do not skip directly to visual components.

Do not claim a phase is complete unless its acceptance criteria are implemented, tested, and demonstrated.

The final framework must make simple configuration screens and HUD elements extremely concise while preserving strong typing, modularity, predictable performance, safe extension, testability, and future compatibility.